/* Modules 16-20. Original study text. [[Term]] or [[Glossary Term|text shown]] creates a glossary popup link. */
M({
  id: 16,
  title: "Environmental Issues and Disclosures",
  summary: "The environmental hazards licensees run into most often, the federal lead-based paint rules, Superfund liability, flood zones, and how to handle disclosure without acting as an expert.",
  objectives: [
    "Identify common residential hazards: lead-based paint, asbestos, radon, mold and carbon monoxide.",
    "Explain the federal lead-based paint disclosure requirements for pre-1978 housing.",
    "Describe underground storage tanks, groundwater issues and septic systems.",
    "Explain Superfund (CERCLA) liability and the role of a Phase I environmental assessment.",
    "Describe a licensee's disclosure duties and when to refer clients to experts."
  ],
  sections: [
    ["The licensee's role", `<p>Licensees are not environmental experts and should never guess whether a property is safe. Your job is to: know the common warning signs, disclose known problems, recommend professional inspections, and make sure required disclosure forms are completed. Saying “there's no radon here” without a test is a misrepresentation; saying “radon is common in this area, and I recommend a test” protects everyone.</p>`],
    ["Lead-based paint", `<p>Lead was banned from residential paint in <strong>1978</strong>. Lead dust and chips can cause serious brain and nervous-system damage, especially in young children. For housing built before 1978, the federal [[lead-based paint disclosure]] rule requires sellers and landlords to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Disclose any known lead-based paint or hazards and give buyers or tenants any available reports.</li>
<li>Provide the EPA pamphlet <em>Protect Your Family From Lead in Your Home</em>.</li>
<li>Include a lead warning statement in the sales contract or lease, signed by the parties.</li>
<li>Give buyers a <strong>10-day opportunity</strong> to conduct a lead inspection or risk assessment (buyers may waive or change this period in writing). Tenants do not get the 10-day period.</li>
<li>Keep the signed disclosure records for <strong>3 years</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Licensees for the seller or landlord must make sure these steps happen and can be held liable if they do not. The rule does not require the seller to test for or remove lead. Contractors disturbing paint in pre-1978 homes must follow EPA lead-safe work practices.</p>`],
    ["Asbestos, radon, mold and indoor air", `<ul>
<li>[[Asbestos]] was widely used in insulation, flooring and siding until the late 1970s. It is dangerous when it becomes <strong>friable</strong> (crumbly) and fibers are inhaled. Often the safest option is <strong>encapsulation</strong> (sealing it) rather than removal, which must be done by licensed professionals.</li>
<li>[[Radon]] is a colorless, odorless radioactive gas produced by natural decay of uranium in soil. It enters through cracks in foundations and is a leading cause of lung cancer. The EPA recommends mitigation when levels are at or above <strong>4 picocuries per liter</strong> (pCi/L). Testing is inexpensive, and mitigation systems are effective.</li>
<li><strong>Mold</strong> grows where there is moisture. Visible mold or a musty smell signals a water problem that must be fixed, and the mold cleaned up.</li>
<li><strong>Carbon monoxide</strong> (CO) is a colorless, odorless gas from faulty furnaces, water heaters and fireplaces. Detectors are required in many jurisdictions.</li>
<li><strong>Urea-formaldehyde</strong> foam insulation and some building materials can release formaldehyde gas, which irritates the eyes and lungs.</li>
<li>Former <strong>methamphetamine labs</strong> can leave toxic residue; many states have cleanup and disclosure rules.</li>
</ul>`],
    ["Water, wells, septic systems and storage tanks", `<p>Groundwater can be contaminated by landfills, leaking tanks, farm chemicals and failing septic systems. Buyers of homes with private <strong>wells</strong> should have the water tested. Homes not on public sewer use a <strong>septic system</strong>; a soil <strong>percolation test</strong> shows whether the soil can absorb wastewater before a new system is installed.</p>
<p>[[Underground storage tanks]] (USTs) hold petroleum or chemicals, such as at gas stations. Leaks can contaminate soil and water. Federal rules require commercial tank owners to register tanks, detect leaks and clean up spills. Small residential heating-oil tanks used on the premises are generally exempt from the federal rules, but an abandoned tank can still be a costly problem.</p>`],
    ["Superfund liability and brownfields", `<p>The Comprehensive Environmental Response, Compensation, and Liability Act ([[CERCLA]]), often called <strong>Superfund</strong>, funds cleanup of hazardous waste sites. Liability under CERCLA is:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Strict</strong>: no need to prove the owner was at fault.</li>
<li><strong>Joint and several</strong>: any one responsible party can be made to pay the entire cost.</li>
<li><strong>Retroactive</strong>: it applies to contamination that happened before the law was passed.</li>
</ul>
<p>Current and past owners can be “potentially responsible parties.” A buyer can qualify for the <strong>innocent landowner</strong> or bona fide prospective purchaser defense by conducting “all appropriate inquiry” before buying, typically a [[Phase I environmental site assessment]]. Commercial lenders usually require one.</p>
<p>[[Brownfields]] are abandoned or underused industrial sites with real or suspected contamination. Federal and state brownfield programs offer grants and liability relief to encourage cleanup and redevelopment.</p>`],
    ["Flood zones, wetlands and disclosure", `<p>FEMA flood maps identify <strong>special flood hazard areas</strong>. If a home with a federally backed or regulated loan is in one, the lender will require flood insurance. Standard homeowners policies do not cover flood damage. Federally protected <strong>wetlands</strong> generally cannot be filled or built on without permits.</p>
<p>Georgia has historically followed <strong>caveat emptor</strong> (“let the buyer beware”) for sellers, but this is limited: sellers may not hide defects or lie, and licensees must disclose known adverse material facts about the property's physical condition. The Georgia REALTORS® forms include a seller's property disclosure statement that most sellers complete. Encourage buyers to get a professional home inspection and any specialized tests (radon, termite, sewer, mold) during their due diligence period.</p>`]
  ],
  terms: [
    ["Lead-based paint disclosure", "Federal rules for pre-1978 housing requiring disclosure, an EPA pamphlet, a warning statement and a 10-day inspection opportunity for buyers."],
    ["Asbestos", "A mineral fiber once used in building materials that is hazardous when friable and inhaled."],
    ["Radon", "A naturally occurring, odorless radioactive gas that can seep into homes; the EPA action level is 4 pCi/L."],
    ["Underground storage tanks", "Buried tanks holding petroleum or chemicals that can leak and contaminate soil and groundwater."],
    ["CERCLA", "The federal Superfund law imposing strict, joint and several, retroactive liability for hazardous waste cleanup."],
    ["Phase I environmental site assessment", "A review of a property's history and condition to identify possible contamination before purchase."],
    ["Brownfields", "Abandoned or underused industrial sites with actual or suspected contamination."]
  ],
  scenarios: [
    ["A 1965 home", "Your buyer client is making an offer on a home built in 1965. Because it was built before 1978, the seller must give the lead-based paint disclosure form and EPA pamphlet, and the contract must include the lead warning statement. Your buyer has 10 days to arrange a lead inspection unless they waive it in writing. You also notice the basement is in an area known for elevated radon, so you recommend a radon test during the due diligence period. You do not tell the buyer the house is safe; you point them to qualified inspectors."]
  ],
  traps: [
    "The lead-based paint rules apply to housing built <strong>before 1978</strong>, not “in or before 1978.”",
    "The seller must disclose known lead hazards but is <em>not</em> required to test for or remove lead.",
    "Buyers get a 10-day inspection opportunity; tenants do not.",
    "CERCLA liability is strict, joint and several, and retroactive. Fault is not required.",
    "Radon is odorless and invisible. The only way to know the level is to test."
  ],
  quiz: [
    ["Federal lead-based paint disclosure rules apply to housing built before:", ["1950", "1970", "1978", "1990"], 2, "The [[lead-based paint disclosure]] rules apply to housing built before 1978."],
    ["How many days do buyers usually have to conduct a lead-based paint inspection?", ["3", "5", "10", "30"], 2, "Buyers get a 10-day opportunity, which can be waived or changed in writing."],
    ["Lead-based paint disclosure records must be kept for:", ["1 year", "3 years", "5 years", "7 years"], 1, "Sellers, landlords and agents must keep the records for 3 years."],
    ["A colorless, odorless radioactive gas that enters homes from the soil is:", ["Carbon monoxide", "Radon", "Formaldehyde", "Asbestos"], 1, "[[Radon]] comes from natural uranium decay in soil."],
    ["Asbestos is most dangerous when it is:", ["Encapsulated", "Painted", "Friable", "Wet"], 2, "Friable (crumbly) asbestos releases fibers that can be inhaled."],
    ["Under CERCLA, liability for cleanup is:", ["Based only on fault", "Strict, joint and several, and retroactive", "Limited to the current owner", "Limited to the original polluter"], 1, "[[CERCLA]] imposes strict, joint and several, retroactive liability."],
    ["A buyer of commercial land wants to qualify for the innocent landowner defense. The buyer should obtain a:", ["Home warranty", "Phase I environmental site assessment", "Certificate of occupancy", "Title insurance policy only"], 1, "A [[Phase I environmental site assessment]] satisfies the “all appropriate inquiry” requirement."],
    ["A test that measures how well soil absorbs water for a septic system is a:", ["Radon test", "Percolation test", "Phase II test", "Pressure test"], 1, "A percolation (perc) test shows whether soil can absorb septic effluent."],
    ["A licensee asked whether a home has mold should:", ["Say no if none is visible", "Disclose what they know and recommend a professional inspection", "Refuse to answer", "Tell the buyer mold is harmless"], 1, "Licensees disclose known facts and refer clients to qualified experts rather than guessing."],
    ["If a home with a federally backed loan is in a special flood hazard area, the lender will require:", ["A radon test", "Flood insurance", "A Phase I assessment", "A larger down payment"], 1, "Flood insurance is required in special flood hazard areas for federally backed or regulated loans."]
  ]
});

M({
  id: 17,
  title: "Closing the Transaction",
  summary: "What happens between contract and keys: preclosing steps, Georgia attorney closings, RESPA and TRID rules, reading a closing statement, and calculating prorations.",
  objectives: [
    "List the main preclosing tasks and who handles each one.",
    "Explain RESPA's ban on kickbacks and its escrow account limits.",
    "Describe the TRID timeline for the Loan Estimate and Closing Disclosure.",
    "Classify items as buyer or seller debits and credits on a closing statement.",
    "Calculate prorations for property taxes and other items."
  ],
  sections: [
    ["Before closing", `<p>Between contract and closing, many things must happen, often within contract deadlines:</p>
<ul>
<li>The buyer completes inspections and negotiates any repairs during the due diligence period.</li>
<li>The lender orders an <strong>appraisal</strong> and finishes underwriting.</li>
<li>The closing attorney or title company performs the <strong>title search</strong> and orders title insurance. Any liens or clouds must be cleared.</li>
<li>A <strong>survey</strong> may be ordered to confirm boundaries and check for encroachments.</li>
<li>The buyer arranges <strong>homeowners insurance</strong>, which the lender requires before funding.</li>
<li>The buyer does a <strong>final walk-through</strong> shortly before closing to confirm the property's condition and that agreed repairs and included items are in place.</li>
</ul>`],
    ["Conducting the closing", `<p>[[Closing]] (settlement) is when the deed is delivered, the purchase price is paid, the loan documents are signed and the transaction is completed. There are two styles:</p>
<ul>
<li>A <strong>face-to-face closing</strong> where the parties sign in person, often at the same table.</li>
<li>An <strong>escrow closing</strong> where a neutral escrow agent collects documents and funds from each party and completes the transaction when all conditions are met. Common in western states.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Georgia uses attorney closings.</strong> A licensed Georgia attorney must conduct or supervise the closing of a real estate transaction. The attorney typically represents the lender, prepares or reviews the deed and closing documents, disburses funds and records the deed and security deed.</p>
<p>The closing agent also handles tax reporting. The sale must be reported to the IRS on <strong>Form 1099-S</strong>. Under the Foreign Investment in Real Property Tax Act (<strong>FIRPTA</strong>), when the seller is a foreign person the buyer generally must withhold 15% of the amount realized and send it to the IRS, with some exceptions for lower-priced homes the buyer will live in.</p>`],
    ["RESPA", `<p>The [[RESPA|Real Estate Settlement Procedures Act]] (RESPA) applies to most residential loans on 1 to 4 family homes made by federally related lenders. Its main rules:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>No kickbacks or unearned fees.</strong> No one may give or accept anything of value for referring settlement business (title, lending, inspections, insurance). A licensee cannot accept a gift card from a lender for each buyer referral.</li>
<li><strong>Affiliated business arrangements</strong> (for example, a brokerage that owns part of a title company) are allowed only with written disclosure and when the consumer is free to choose another provider.</li>
<li>A seller cannot require the buyer to use a particular title insurance company as a condition of sale.</li>
<li>Lenders may require an <strong>escrow (impound) account</strong> for taxes and insurance, but the cushion is limited to one-sixth (two months) of the annual amount.</li>
<li>Lenders must give a list of housing counselors and, for purchase loans, a home-buying information booklet.</li>
</ul>`],
    ["TRID disclosures", `<p>Under the TILA-RESPA Integrated Disclosure rule:</p>
<ul>
<li>The [[Loan Estimate]] must be delivered within <strong>3 business days</strong> after the lender receives a loan application.</li>
<li>The [[Closing Disclosure]] must be received by the borrower at least <strong>3 business days before closing</strong>. It shows the final loan terms and every closing cost.</li>
<li>A new 3-day waiting period is required if the APR increases beyond a set tolerance, the loan product changes, or a prepayment penalty is added. Most other changes do not restart the clock.</li>
</ul>`],
    ["Reading a closing statement", `<p>A closing statement lists each party's [[debit|debits]] (amounts the party owes or is charged) and [[credit|credits]] (amounts the party receives or is credited).</p>
<div class="tbl-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Item</th><th>Buyer</th><th>Seller</th></tr>
<tr><td>Purchase price</td><td>Debit</td><td>Credit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Earnest money</td><td>Credit</td><td>Not shown (already part of the price)</td></tr>
<tr><td>New loan</td><td>Credit</td><td>Not shown</td></tr>
<tr><td>Payoff of seller's existing loan</td><td>Not shown</td><td>Debit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Brokerage commission</td><td>Debit only if agreed</td><td>Debit if agreed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Unpaid taxes the seller owes for the year so far (paid in arrears)</td><td>Credit</td><td>Debit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Taxes the seller prepaid beyond closing</td><td>Debit</td><td>Credit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Assumed loan</td><td>Credit</td><td>Debit</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>A simple rule: when an item is a <strong>double entry</strong> (it moves between buyer and seller), it is a debit to one and a credit to the other for the same amount. A <strong>single entry</strong> item, like the seller's loan payoff or the buyer's new loan, appears on only one side.</p>`],
    ["Prorations", `<p>[[Proration]] divides expenses and income between buyer and seller according to how long each owned the property during the period. Common prorated items: property taxes, HOA dues, rents and interest on assumed loans.</p>
<ol>
<li>Decide who owns the closing day. The contract controls; on most exams, the seller pays through the day before closing and the buyer owns the day of closing, unless the question says otherwise.</li>
<li>Find the daily rate using either a <strong>360-day year</strong> (banker's year: 12 months of 30 days) or a <strong>365-day year</strong> (exact days), as the question says.</li>
<li>Multiply the daily (or monthly) rate by the number of days the party owes.</li>
<li>Decide who is debited: if the bill is <strong>paid in arrears</strong> (after the period, as with Georgia property taxes), the seller has not paid yet and owes the buyer for the seller's share: <strong>debit seller, credit buyer</strong>. If the seller <strong>prepaid</strong> (as with HOA dues paid in advance), the buyer owes the seller for the rest of the period: <strong>debit buyer, credit seller</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Example (360-day year):</strong> Annual taxes are $2,880, not yet paid. Closing is April 16; the seller owns through April 15. Monthly taxes = $2,880 ÷ 12 = $240; daily = $240 ÷ 30 = $8. The seller owes 3 months + 15 days = (3 × $240) + (15 × $8) = $720 + $120 = <strong>$840</strong>. Debit the seller $840 and credit the buyer $840.</p>
<p>Rent collected in advance for the month of closing belongs partly to the buyer: credit the buyer and debit the seller for the buyer's days. Security deposits transfer in full to the buyer as the new landlord.</p>`]
  ],
  terms: [
    ["Closing", "The completion of a real estate transaction, when the deed is delivered and funds are disbursed; also called settlement."],
    ["RESPA", "The Real Estate Settlement Procedures Act, which bans kickbacks and regulates closing practices for most residential loans."],
    ["Loan Estimate", "A TRID disclosure of estimated loan terms and costs, due within 3 business days of application."],
    ["Closing Disclosure", "A TRID disclosure of final loan terms and costs that the borrower must receive 3 business days before closing."],
    ["Debit", "A charge on a closing statement: an amount a party owes."],
    ["Credit", "An amount on a closing statement that a party receives or is credited."],
    ["Proration", "Dividing expenses or income between buyer and seller based on their period of ownership."]
  ],
  scenarios: [
    ["The referral gift", "A mortgage company offers a $100 restaurant card to a new licensee for every buyer she sends them. Accepting it would violate RESPA's ban on referral fees, even though the amount is small. She can still recommend lenders she trusts; she just cannot be paid for the referral. She should also tell clients they are free to choose any lender."]
  ],
  traps: [
    "Earnest money is a <strong>credit to the buyer</strong> only. It does not appear on the seller's side because it is already part of the purchase price.",
    "Taxes paid in arrears: debit the seller, credit the buyer. Prepaid items: debit the buyer, credit the seller.",
    "A banker's year has 360 days and 30-day months. Use 365 days only when the question says so.",
    "RESPA bans referral fees between settlement service providers, no matter how small.",
    "The Closing Disclosure must be <em>received</em> 3 business days before closing, not just sent."
  ],
  quiz: [
    ["Which closing statement entry is a credit to the buyer?", ["Purchase price", "Earnest money", "Prepaid taxes reimbursed to seller", "Homeowners insurance premium"], 1, "The buyer already paid the earnest money, so it is credited toward what the buyer owes."],
    ["Property taxes for the year are paid in arrears. At closing, the seller's share of unpaid taxes is:", ["Debited to the buyer, credited to the seller", "Debited to the seller, credited to the buyer", "Debited to both", "Not prorated"], 1, "The seller has not paid their share yet, so the seller is debited and the buyer credited."],
    ["The seller's existing mortgage payoff appears as:", ["A credit to the seller", "A debit to the seller only", "A debit to the buyer", "A credit to the buyer"], 1, "The payoff is a single entry: a debit to the seller."],
    ["Under TRID, the Loan Estimate must be provided within how many business days of application?", ["1", "3", "7", "10"], 1, "The [[Loan Estimate]] is due within 3 business days of the application."],
    ["RESPA prohibits:", ["Seller-paid closing costs", "Kickbacks and unearned referral fees", "Escrow accounts", "Attorney closings"], 1, "[[RESPA]] bans kickbacks and fees for referrals of settlement business."],
    ["Under RESPA, the cushion a lender may hold in an escrow account is limited to:", ["One month of payments", "One-sixth of the annual amount", "Half the annual amount", "There is no limit"], 1, "The cushion is limited to one-sixth (two months) of annual escrow disbursements."],
    ["In Georgia, real estate closings are conducted or supervised by:", ["The listing broker", "A licensed Georgia attorney", "The county clerk", "The buyer's lender only"], 1, "Georgia requires closings to be conducted or supervised by a licensed attorney."],
    ["Annual taxes of $3,600 are unpaid. Using a 360-day year, the seller owes for 2 months and 10 days. The proration is:", ["$600", "$700", "$720", "$1,000"], 1, "Monthly $300; daily $10. (2 × $300) + (10 × $10) = $700."],
    ["Which form reports the sale of real estate to the IRS?", ["W-2", "1099-S", "1040", "1098"], 1, "Settlement agents report sale proceeds on Form 1099-S."],
    ["When a seller is a foreign person, FIRPTA generally requires the buyer to withhold:", ["5% of the price", "10% of the gain", "15% of the amount realized", "Nothing"], 2, "FIRPTA generally requires withholding 15% of the amount realized, with some exceptions."]
  ]
});

M({
  id: 18,
  title: "Real Estate Math",
  summary: "Every formula you need for the exam, with worked examples: percentages, commission, area and volume, loans and interest, taxes, prorations, appraisal and investment math.",
  objectives: [
    "Solve any percentage problem using the Part = Total × Rate formula.",
    "Calculate commissions, splits, seller's net and profit or loss.",
    "Measure area, volume and acreage, and convert between units.",
    "Calculate LTV, points, interest and the first month's principal payment.",
    "Apply property tax, proration, depreciation and cap-rate formulas."
  ],
  sections: [
    ["The one formula behind most problems", `<p>Most real estate math is a [[percentage formula]] problem:</p>
<p><strong>Part = Total × Rate</strong> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <strong>Total = Part ÷ Rate</strong> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <strong>Rate = Part ÷ Total</strong></p>
<p>Change percentages to decimals by moving the decimal two places left (6% = 0.06; 5.5% = 0.055). Identify which two of the three numbers you have, then solve for the third.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Commission</strong>: commission = price × rate.</li>
<li><strong>Taxes</strong>: tax = taxable value × tax rate.</li>
<li><strong>Interest</strong>: interest = principal × rate.</li>
<li><strong>Income property</strong>: NOI = value × cap rate (IRV).</li>
</ul>
<p>Use the <a href="#math">math calculators</a> to check your work after solving by hand.</p>`],
    ["Commission, splits and seller's net", `<p><strong>Commission split example:</strong> A home sells for $360,000 at 6%. Total commission: $360,000 × 0.06 = $21,600. The listing and selling firms split 50/50: $10,800 each. The selling agent's split with her broker is 70/30: $10,800 × 0.70 = <strong>$7,560</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Seller's net:</strong> A seller wants to walk away with $285,000 after a 5% commission. The net is 95% of the price, so price = $285,000 ÷ 0.95 = <strong>$300,000</strong>. (Do not add 5% to $285,000; that gives the wrong answer.) This is the [[seller's net]] formula.</p>
<p><strong>Profit or loss</strong> is always based on what the owner <em>paid</em>: percent profit = (sale price − cost) ÷ cost. Bought for $160,000 and sold for $200,000: $40,000 ÷ $160,000 = <strong>25% profit</strong>.</p>`],
    ["Area, volume and land measurement", `<ul>
<li>Rectangle or square: area = length × width.</li>
<li>Triangle: area = ½ × base × height. A triangle with a 100-ft base and 80-ft height = 4,000 sq ft.</li>
<li>Irregular shapes: split into rectangles and triangles, then add.</li>
<li>Volume: length × width × height. A room 12 × 15 × 9 ft = 1,620 cubic feet.</li>
<li>Cubic yards: divide cubic feet by <strong>27</strong> (3 × 3 × 3). Square yards: divide square feet by <strong>9</strong>.</li>
<li>Acres: divide square feet by <strong>43,560</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<p>[[Front foot|Front feet]] measure the side of a lot that faces the street. The first number in a lot description is usually the frontage: an 80 × 150 lot has 80 front feet. At $500 per front foot, it is worth $40,000.</p>`],
    ["Loans and interest", `<ul>
<li><strong>LTV</strong>: loan ÷ value (lower of price or appraisal). $216,000 ÷ $240,000 = 90%.</li>
<li><strong>Points</strong>: 1 point = 1% of the <em>loan</em>. 2.5 points on $280,000 = $7,000.</li>
<li><strong>[[Equity]]</strong>: market value − loan balance. $350,000 − $210,000 = $140,000.</li>
<li><strong>Simple interest</strong>: I = P × R × T. Annual interest ÷ 12 = monthly interest.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>First payment split:</strong> A $300,000 loan at 6% for 30 years has a principal-and-interest payment of $1,798.65. Month 1 interest = $300,000 × 0.06 ÷ 12 = $1,500. Principal paid = $1,798.65 − $1,500 = <strong>$298.65</strong>. New balance = $299,701.35. Each month, interest is calculated on the new, lower balance.</p>
<p><strong>Qualifying ratios:</strong> A buyer earns $6,000 a month. A lender using 28/36 limits housing expense to $6,000 × 0.28 = $1,680 and total debts to $6,000 × 0.36 = $2,160.</p>`],
    ["Taxes and prorations", `<p><strong>Property tax:</strong> taxable value × rate. With mills, divide by 1,000. Georgia example: market value $300,000 × 40% = $120,000 assessed; minus $2,000 exemption = $118,000; × 30 mills (0.030) = $3,540.</p>
<p><strong>Georgia transfer tax:</strong> $1 per $1,000 of price. A $325,000 sale = $325.</p>
<p><strong>Prorations:</strong> find the daily or monthly amount, multiply by the days owed, then decide debit and credit (see Module 17). Annual taxes $3,650 paid in arrears, 365-day year, closing July 1, seller owns through June 30 (181 days): $3,650 ÷ 365 = $10 a day × 181 = <strong>$1,810</strong>, debited to the seller and credited to the buyer.</p>`],
    ["Appraisal and investment math", `<ul>
<li><strong>Cap rate</strong>: Value = NOI ÷ Rate. NOI $45,000 at 9% = $500,000.</li>
<li><strong>GRM</strong>: Value = monthly rent × GRM.</li>
<li><strong>Straight-line depreciation</strong>: cost ÷ useful life. A $300,000 building with a 50-year life loses $6,000 a year; after 10 years, $60,000 (20%).</li>
<li><strong>Cost approach</strong>: cost new − depreciation + land.</li>
<li>[[Appreciation]] compounds year by year: $200,000 rising 4% a year for 2 years = $200,000 × 1.04 × 1.04 = $216,320.</li>
</ul>`]
  ],
  terms: [
    ["Percentage formula", "Part = Total × Rate; the basis for commission, tax, interest and cap-rate problems."],
    ["Seller's net", "The amount a seller keeps after costs; price needed = desired net ÷ (1 − commission rate)."],
    ["Front foot", "A measurement of the side of a lot that faces the street."],
    ["Equity", "The owner's interest in a property: market value minus debts against it."],
    ["Appreciation", "An increase in a property's value over time."],
    ["Simple interest", "Interest calculated as principal × rate × time."],
    ["Cubic yard", "A volume unit equal to 27 cubic feet."]
  ],
  scenarios: [
    ["Pricing to hit a net", "Sellers owe $142,000 on their mortgage, expect $6,500 in other closing costs, want $60,000 cash at closing, and agreed to a 6% commission. Their required net before commission is $142,000 + $6,500 + $60,000 = $208,500. Price = $208,500 ÷ 0.94 = $221,808.51, so they would need to sell for about $222,000. Showing the math helps sellers understand why the price matters."]
  ],
  traps: [
    "For seller's net, <strong>divide</strong> by (1 − commission rate). Adding the commission percentage to the net gives a price that is too low.",
    "Profit percentage is based on the original <em>cost</em>, not the sale price.",
    "Points are a percentage of the loan, not the price.",
    "Divide cubic feet by 27 for cubic yards, and square feet by 9 for square yards.",
    "Read which year the proration uses: 360 days (30-day months) or 365 days."
  ],
  quiz: [
    ["A home sells for $425,000 at a 5.5% commission. What is the total commission?", ["$21,250", "$23,375", "$23,750", "$25,500"], 1, "$425,000 × 0.055 = $23,375."],
    ["A rectangular lot measures 132 feet by 330 feet. How large is it?", ["Half an acre", "1 acre", "2 acres", "10 acres"], 1, "132 × 330 = 43,560 sq ft, exactly 1 [[acre]]."],
    ["Sellers want to net $190,000 after paying a 5% commission (no other costs). What must the price be?", ["$199,500", "$200,000", "$195,000", "$180,500"], 1, "Price = $190,000 ÷ 0.95 = $200,000."],
    ["An investor bought a house for $160,000 and sold it for $200,000. What was the percentage of profit?", ["20%", "25%", "40%", "80%"], 1, "Profit $40,000 ÷ cost $160,000 = 25%."],
    ["The price is $250,000, the appraisal is $240,000, and the loan is $216,000. What is the LTV?", ["86.4%", "90%", "84%", "95%"], 1, "Use the lower value: $216,000 ÷ $240,000 = 90%."],
    ["What is the first month's interest on a $200,000 loan at 6% annual interest?", ["$12,000", "$1,200", "$1,000", "$100"], 2, "$200,000 × 0.06 = $12,000 a year ÷ 12 = $1,000."],
    ["A home's taxable value is $120,000 and the tax rate is 25 mills. What is the annual tax?", ["$300", "$3,000", "$30,000", "$2,500"], 1, "25 mills = 0.025. $120,000 × 0.025 = $3,000."],
    ["Annual taxes of $3,650 are unpaid. Using a 365-day year, the seller owns the first 181 days. What is the proration?", ["$1,810 debit seller, credit buyer", "$1,810 debit buyer, credit seller", "$1,840 debit seller, credit buyer", "$1,825 debit buyer, credit seller"], 0, "$3,650 ÷ 365 = $10 a day × 181 = $1,810. Taxes in arrears: debit the seller, credit the buyer."],
    ["A building produces NOI of $45,000. At a 9% cap rate, its value is:", ["$405,000", "$450,000", "$500,000", "$540,000"], 2, "Value = NOI ÷ Rate = $45,000 ÷ 0.09 = $500,000."],
    ["A driveway is 50 ft long, 12 ft wide and 6 inches thick. About how many cubic yards of concrete are needed?", ["11.1", "22.2", "33.3", "300"], 0, "50 × 12 × 0.5 = 300 cubic feet ÷ 27 = 11.1 [[cubic yard|cubic yards]]."]
  ]
});

M({
  id: 19,
  title: "State Requirements and Licensing Steps (Georgia)",
  summary: "The step-by-step path to a Georgia salesperson license: eligibility, the 75-hour course, the state exam, background check, application, broker affiliation, post-license education and renewal, plus Georgia-specific rules that appear on the exam.",
  objectives: [
    "List the eligibility requirements for a Georgia salesperson license.",
    "Describe the pre-license course, the state exam and the background check.",
    "Explain how to activate a license by affiliating with a broker.",
    "Describe post-license education, renewal and continuing education requirements.",
    "Recall Georgia-specific rules that are tested on the state portion of the exam."
  ],
  sections: [
    ["Confirm before you act", `<p><strong>Important:</strong> Licensing rules, fees, hours and exam details change. The information in this module reflects general Georgia requirements as understood when it was written. Before you enroll, schedule the exam or apply, confirm every detail with the <strong>Georgia Real Estate Commission</strong> (GREC) at <strong>grec.state.ga.us</strong> and in the current candidate handbook from the state's testing vendor.</p>
<p>The [[Georgia Real Estate Commission]] licenses and regulates real estate licensees in Georgia under the Georgia Real Estate License Law (O.C.G.A. Title 43, Chapter 40) and the commission's rules. It also enforces the law, investigates complaints and disciplines licensees.</p>`],
    ["Step 1: Meet the eligibility requirements", `<ul>
<li>Be at least <strong>18 years old</strong>.</li>
<li>Have a <strong>high school diploma or GED</strong> (or an equivalent recognized by the commission).</li>
<li>Be lawfully present in the United States.</li>
<li>Meet the commission's character standards. Applicants with a criminal history, a past license revocation, or certain judgments may need the commission to review their record before they can be licensed. Being honest on your application is essential; failing to disclose is itself grounds for denial.</li>
</ul>`],
    ["Step 2: Complete the 75-hour pre-license course", `<p>Georgia requires a <strong>75-hour salesperson pre-license course</strong> from a school approved by the commission (in a classroom or online). You must pass the course's final exam. Your school issues a certificate of completion, which you need to sit for the state exam. A study aid like this dashboard does not count toward the 75 hours.</p>
<p>Some applicants (for example, those with certain college real estate coursework or a license from another state) may qualify for different education rules. Ask GREC.</p>`],
    ["Step 3: Pass the state licensing exam", `<p>The exam is computer-based and given at test centers by the state's contracted testing vendor (currently PSI). Expect two portions:</p>
<ul>
<li>A <strong>national portion</strong> on general real estate principles and practices (about 100 scored questions).</li>
<li>A <strong>state portion</strong> on Georgia license law, rules and practices (about 50 scored questions).</li>
</ul>
<p>You should plan for about four hours of testing. You must pass both portions; if you fail one, you generally retake only that portion within a set time. Check the current candidate handbook for exact question counts, time limits, passing scores, fees, ID requirements and what calculators are permitted.</p>`],
    ["Step 4: Background check", `<p>Georgia requires applicants to provide a <strong>criminal background report</strong>. Applicants are typically instructed to obtain a Georgia Crime Information Center (GCIC) report or complete fingerprinting through the commission's approved process, and the report must be recent (a 60-day window has commonly applied). Follow the exact instructions in the current candidate handbook, since the process has changed over time.</p>`],
    ["Step 5: Apply and affiliate with a broker", `<p>After passing the exam, submit your license application and fee to the commission. Georgia has required applicants to apply within a set time after passing (historically three months); if you miss the deadline you may need to retake the exam.</p>
<p>A salesperson license is <strong>active</strong> only when you are affiliated with a licensed Georgia broker who holds your license. Without a broker, your license can be issued or placed on <strong>inactive</strong> status, and you cannot perform brokerage activities. Interview several brokerages: ask about training, mentoring, commission splits, fees and the kind of business they do.</p>`],
    ["Step 6: Post-license education and renewal", `<ul>
<li><strong>Post-license course:</strong> new Georgia salespersons must complete a <strong>25-hour post-license course</strong> within the <strong>first year</strong> after the license is issued. If you do not, your license can lapse.</li>
<li><strong>Renewal:</strong> Georgia licenses renew every <strong>4 years</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Continuing education:</strong> <strong>36 hours</strong> of approved continuing education are required for each renewal period, including required hours on Georgia license law. The commission sets which topics are mandatory, so check current rules.</li>
<li><strong>Upgrading to broker:</strong> Georgia brokers must generally be at least 21, have held an active license for at least 3 of the previous 5 years, complete a 60-hour broker course, and pass the broker exam.</li>
</ul>`],
    ["Georgia rules that show up on the exam", `<p>This list reviews Georgia-specific points covered elsewhere in the course and adds a few license-law basics. Confirm details in your pre-license course text.</p>
<ul>
<li>Agency relationships are governed by [[BRRETA]] (Module 8). Client relationships require a written brokerage engagement, and licensees must disclose whom they represent.</li>
<li>Licensees must disclose known adverse material facts about the property's physical condition and immediate neighborhood (Module 8).</li>
<li>Earnest money and other trust funds must go into the broker's <strong>designated trust (escrow) account</strong> within the time set by commission rules. Commingling is prohibited (Module 10).</li>
<li>All advertising must clearly identify the brokerage firm. A licensee buying or selling property for themselves must disclose in writing that they hold a license.</li>
<li>A salesperson may be paid only by the broker who holds their license.</li>
<li>Property is assessed for tax at 40% of fair market value (Module 7). The transfer tax is $1 per $1,000 (Module 11).</li>
<li>Georgia uses security deeds and nonjudicial foreclosure with sales on the first Tuesday of the month (Module 12).</li>
<li>Closings are conducted or supervised by Georgia attorneys (Module 17).</li>
<li>Adverse possession generally takes 20 years, or 7 years with color of title (Module 11).</li>
<li>The commission may <strong>reprimand, fine, suspend or revoke</strong> a license for violations. A state recovery fund may compensate consumers harmed by a licensee's fraud or misconduct after a court judgment, with limits.</li>
</ul>`]
  ],
  terms: [
    ["Georgia Real Estate Commission", "The state agency (GREC) that licenses, regulates and disciplines Georgia real estate licensees."],
    ["BRRETA", "Georgia's Brokerage Relationships in Real Estate Transactions Act, governing agency and brokerage relationships."],
    ["Pre-license course", "The 75-hour commission-approved course required before taking the Georgia salesperson exam."],
    ["Post-license course", "A 25-hour course new Georgia salespersons must complete within the first year of licensure."],
    ["Continuing education", "Approved courses (36 hours per 4-year renewal in Georgia) required to keep a license."],
    ["Active license", "A license held by a broker so the licensee may perform brokerage activities."],
    ["Inactive license", "A license not held by a broker; the licensee may not perform brokerage activities."]
  ],
  scenarios: [
    ["Planning the path", "Jordan, 24, finishes a 75-hour online course in March, passes the school's final exam, and schedules the state exam. Jordan passes the national portion but misses the state portion by two questions, reviews license law, and passes the state portion on the retake. Jordan then interviews three brokerages, chooses one with a structured training program, and applies for the license with the broker's affiliation so it issues as active. Jordan schedules the 25-hour post-license course right away so it is finished well within the first year."]
  ],
  traps: [
    "A salesperson cannot work independently. An active license must be held by a broker.",
    "The 25-hour post-license course is due within the <strong>first year</strong>, not at the first 4-year renewal.",
    "Pre-license (75 hours) is before the exam; post-license (25 hours) is after licensing; continuing education (36 hours) is for each renewal.",
    "Licensees selling their own property must disclose that they are licensed.",
    "When this course and the commission disagree, the commission's current rules win."
  ],
  quiz: [
    ["Which agency licenses and regulates real estate licensees in Georgia?", ["Georgia REALTORS®", "Georgia Real Estate Commission", "HUD", "The county clerk"], 1, "The [[Georgia Real Estate Commission]] issues and regulates licenses. Georgia REALTORS® is a trade association."],
    ["How many hours of pre-license education does Georgia require for a salesperson?", ["25", "36", "60", "75"], 3, "Georgia requires a 75-hour approved salesperson pre-license course."],
    ["New Georgia salespersons must complete a 25-hour post-license course within:", ["30 days", "6 months", "The first year of licensure", "4 years"], 2, "The 25-hour post-license course is due within the first year after the license is issued."],
    ["How often are Georgia real estate licenses renewed?", ["Every year", "Every 2 years", "Every 4 years", "Every 5 years"], 2, "Georgia licenses renew every 4 years."],
    ["How many hours of continuing education are required per Georgia renewal period?", ["12", "24", "36", "75"], 2, "Georgia requires 36 hours of continuing education per 4-year renewal period."],
    ["A salesperson's license is active only when:", ["The salesperson pays an extra fee", "It is held by a licensed Georgia broker", "The salesperson joins an association", "The salesperson has sold one home"], 1, "An [[active license]] must be held by a sponsoring broker."],
    ["The Georgia law governing agency relationships is:", ["RESPA", "BRRETA", "CERCLA", "FIRPTA"], 1, "[[BRRETA]] governs brokerage relationships in Georgia."],
    ["A Georgia licensee is selling a rental home they own. They must:", ["Use a different brokerage", "Disclose in writing that they hold a license", "Get commission approval", "Sell only to other licensees"], 1, "Licensees must disclose their licensed status when buying or selling for themselves."],
    ["The minimum age for a Georgia salesperson license is:", ["16", "18", "21", "25"], 1, "Salesperson applicants must be at least 18; brokers generally at least 21."],
    ["Which statement about the Georgia salesperson exam is true?", ["It has only a state portion", "It has a national portion and a state portion", "It is given only on paper", "It can be skipped with a college degree"], 1, "The exam includes a national portion and a Georgia state portion; both must be passed."]
  ]
});

M({
  id: 20,
  title: "Final Exam Prep",
  summary: "How to study in the final weeks, strategies for tricky multiple-choice questions, a one-page formula and mnemonic review, and an exam-day checklist, followed by a mixed review quiz.",
  objectives: [
    "Build a final-weeks study routine using practice exams and the weak-area tracker.",
    "Apply strategies for “EXCEPT,” “NOT” and “best answer” questions.",
    "Recall the most-tested mnemonics and formulas.",
    "Prepare a checklist for exam day.",
    "Manage time so every question gets an answer."
  ],
  sections: [
    ["How to use your final weeks", `<ul>
<li><strong>Take a full timed practice exam</strong> early to find your baseline. Take another each week.</li>
<li><strong>Review every missed question</strong>, not just your score. Read the explanation and the related lesson section.</li>
<li><strong>Use the weak-area tracker</strong> to choose what to study. Your lowest modules give the biggest score gains.</li>
<li><strong>Study in short daily sessions</strong> with flashcards. Spacing out practice works better than one long cram session.</li>
<li><strong>Do math by hand</strong>, then check with the calculators. Many people lose points on math they could have mastered.</li>
<li><strong>Review state law last</strong>, close to exam day, because it is detailed and easy to forget.</li>
</ul>`],
    ["Strategies for multiple-choice questions", `<ul>
<li><strong>Read the last line first</strong> to know what the question is asking, then read the details.</li>
<li><strong>Watch for [[qualifier words]]</strong> like EXCEPT, NOT, ALWAYS, NEVER, BEST and FIRST. An “EXCEPT” question asks for the one false answer.</li>
<li><strong>Eliminate wrong answers</strong>. Getting down to two choices doubles your odds.</li>
<li><strong>Beware of [[distractor|distractors]]</strong>: answers that are true statements but do not answer the question asked.</li>
<li><strong>Answer the question as written</strong>, using national rules on the national portion and Georgia rules on the state portion. Do not add facts that are not in the question.</li>
<li><strong>Don't change answers without a clear reason.</strong> Your first instinct is usually right unless you misread the question.</li>
<li><strong>Pace yourself</strong>: about one minute per question. Flag hard questions, move on, and come back.</li>
<li><strong>Never leave a question blank.</strong> There is no penalty for guessing.</li>
</ul>`],
    ["Mnemonics to know cold", `<div class="tbl-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Mnemonic</th><th>Stands for</th><th>Module</th></tr>
<tr><td>[[PETE]]</td><td>Police power, Eminent domain, Taxation, Escheat</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>[[MARIA]]</td><td>Method of annexation, Adaptation, Relationship, Intention, Agreement (fixture tests)</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>[[PTTI]]</td><td>Possession, Time, Title, Interest (joint tenancy unities)</td><td>5</td></tr>
<tr><td>[[OLD CAR]]</td><td>Obedience, Loyalty, Disclosure, Confidentiality, Accounting, Reasonable care</td><td>8</td></tr>
<tr><td>[[DUST]]</td><td>Demand, Utility, Scarcity, Transferability</td><td>13</td></tr>
<tr><td>[[IRV]]</td><td>Income = Rate × Value</td><td>13</td></tr>
<tr><td>CBS / CPA</td><td>Comp Better Subtract / Comp Poorer Add</td><td>13</td></tr>
<tr><td>ACRT</td><td>Avoid, Control, Retain, Transfer (risk)</td><td>14</td></tr>
</table></div>`],
    ["Formula sheet", `<div class="tbl-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Problem</th><th>Formula</th></tr>
<tr><td>Any percentage</td><td>Part = Total × Rate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Seller's net price</td><td>Net ÷ (1 − commission rate)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Profit %</td><td>(Sale − Cost) ÷ Cost</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acres</td><td>Square feet ÷ 43,560</td></tr>
<tr><td>Section acreage</td><td>640 × each fraction</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cubic yards</td><td>Cubic feet ÷ 27</td></tr>
<tr><td>LTV</td><td>Loan ÷ lesser of price or appraisal</td></tr>
<tr><td>Points</td><td>Loan × 1% per point</td></tr>
<tr><td>Monthly interest</td><td>Balance × annual rate ÷ 12</td></tr>
<tr><td>Property tax</td><td>(Assessed value − exemptions) × rate (mills ÷ 1,000)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Value (income)</td><td>NOI ÷ cap rate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Value (GRM)</td><td>Monthly rent × GRM</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cost approach</td><td>Cost new − depreciation + land</td></tr>
</table></div>`],
    ["Exam-day checklist", `<ul>
<li>Confirm your appointment time, test-center address and the ID requirements in the candidate handbook (names must match exactly).</li>
<li>Bring your pre-license course completion certificate and any background report the handbook requires.</li>
<li>Check which calculator is allowed. Testing centers commonly permit only a silent, non-programmable calculator, or provide one on screen.</li>
<li>Arrive early. Late arrivals may be turned away and lose their fee.</li>
<li>Get a full night of sleep and eat beforehand. A tired brain makes reading mistakes.</li>
<li>During the exam: breathe, read carefully, flag and return, and answer every question.</li>
</ul>
<p>After passing, you will receive instructions for applying for your license. If you do not pass one portion, review your score report to see which content areas need work and use this dashboard's weak-area tracker before retaking it.</p>`]
  ],
  terms: [
    ["Qualifier words", "Words like EXCEPT, NOT, ALWAYS and BEST that change what a test question is asking."],
    ["Distractor", "A wrong answer choice designed to look correct, often a true statement that does not answer the question."],
    ["PETE", "Mnemonic for the four government powers: Police power, Eminent domain, Taxation, Escheat."],
    ["MARIA", "Mnemonic for the fixture tests: Method, Adaptation, Relationship, Intention, Agreement."],
    ["PTTI", "Mnemonic for the four unities of joint tenancy: Possession, Time, Title, Interest."],
    ["OLD CAR", "Mnemonic for fiduciary duties: Obedience, Loyalty, Disclosure, Confidentiality, Accounting, Reasonable care."],
    ["DUST", "Mnemonic for the characteristics of value: Demand, Utility, Scarcity, Transferability."],
    ["IRV", "The income capitalization formula: Income = Rate × Value."]
  ],
  scenarios: [
    ["Two weeks out", "Sam scored 64% on a first practice exam. The topic breakdown showed financing at 40% and math at 50%. Sam spent the next week on Modules 12 and 18 (re-reading, flashcards, quizzes until scoring 80%+, and working every calculator by hand), then took a second practice exam and scored 74%. In the final week Sam reviewed Georgia rules and took a third exam, scoring 79%. Targeted review of weak areas raised the score faster than rereading everything."]
  ],
  traps: [
    "“All of the following EXCEPT” questions ask for the one answer that is <em>false</em>.",
    "A true statement is not necessarily the correct answer; it must answer the question asked.",
    "On the national portion, answer using general principles; save Georgia-specific rules for the state portion.",
    "Do not spend five minutes on one hard question. Flag it and come back.",
    "Unanswered questions are always wrong. Guess if you must."
  ],
  quiz: [
    ["All of the following are fiduciary duties EXCEPT:", ["Loyalty", "Confidentiality", "Obedience", "Guaranteeing the sale price"], 3, "The fiduciary duties are [[OLD CAR]]. Agents do not guarantee prices."],
    ["Which is NOT one of the government's four powers over private property?", ["Police power", "Eminent domain", "Escheat", "Adverse possession"], 3, "[[PETE]]: police power, eminent domain, taxation, escheat. Adverse possession is a private claim."],
    ["Which test for a fixture controls when the parties have a written agreement?", ["Method of annexation", "Adaptation", "Agreement", "Relationship"], 2, "In [[MARIA]], a written agreement overrides the other tests."],
    ["Which deed gives the buyer the most protection?", ["Quitclaim", "Special warranty", "General warranty", "Bargain and sale"], 2, "A general warranty deed covers title defects from any time."],
    ["A buyer signs a contract to purchase. Before closing, the buyer holds:", ["Legal title", "Equitable title", "A life estate", "A leasehold"], 1, "Signing the sales contract gives the buyer equitable title."],
    ["Which listing pays the broker even if the owner finds the buyer?", ["Open", "Exclusive agency", "Exclusive right to sell", "Net"], 2, "Only an exclusive-right-to-sell listing pays the broker regardless of who sells."],
    ["Which approach to value is most useful for an apartment building?", ["Sales comparison", "Cost", "Income", "Gross rent multiplier only"], 2, "Income-producing property is best valued with the income approach ([[IRV]])."],
    ["An agent directs a buyer away from a neighborhood because of the buyer's religion. This is:", ["Blockbusting", "Steering", "Redlining", "Puffing"], 1, "Steering directs people toward or away from areas based on a protected class."],
    ["On the exam, a question you cannot answer should be:", ["Left blank", "Flagged and answered with your best guess", "Reported to the proctor", "Skipped permanently"], 1, "There is no penalty for guessing. Flag it, guess, and return if time allows."],
    ["Lead-based paint disclosures are required for housing built before:", ["1960", "1978", "1988", "2000"], 1, "The federal rules cover housing built before 1978."]
  ]
});
